<p>The European Union and Australia announced the final text of a long-delayed free trade agreement in Canberra on March 24, alongside new pacts on defense, security and, according to Australian reporting, scientific research. European Commission President Ursula von der Leyen and Australian Prime Minister Anthony Albanese said the package is meant to deepen economic ties and build resilience at a time of wider trade and geopolitical uncertainty. The agreement revives talks that dragged on for years and broke down in 2023 over agricultural access and protected food names. Several reports said both sides were also trying to diversify trade links, reduce dependence on China and limit exposure to tariffs imposed by President Trump on allies, while EU officials cast the deal as support for a rules-based trading order and greater strategic autonomy for Europe.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/JXzOmqlIwBe6tsBzVk5sXMGwczgozjE_eacIsJoQmQEOfdSvx4q6vJqoApC8kKgcFgLsBV8VQbMt5yvxu47HAQEMn62gHz8x5UAxyUCgV0Gcevn8vqxLTuWWdYdodc0eCDPOhdwGP3mt4EWUKCn6UKN4LKmScJn6D9NZpt-SEjUryu-Fz3p2k3PJMrrbwEuk14-cIWGWwMFpETGEJ7CKROW7RUZP6utmF2HMVw6FuXxwbVI0QBJ3oc7Fx88iRFhjiyPL1FX2yLITXIOADA' alt='European Commission President Ursula von der Leyen, left, and Australian Prime Minister Anthony Albanese shake hands after signing a joint statement during a ceremony at Parliament House in Canberra, Tuesday, March 24, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Export boost: Von der Leyen said the free trade deal could raise export volumes by as much as one third, presenting it as a practical response to geopolitical and economic shocks.</li><li>Security logic: Von der Leyen said modern threats no longer stop at distance, arguing the linked pacts on cyber and defense reflect how technology lets malicious actors reach across borders.</li><li>Farm concerns: Australian farm groups warned that limited EU quotas for beef and lamb could leave agriculture worse off, with the National Farmers Federation saying no deal would be better than a bad one.</li><li>Broader EU message: In Canberra, von der Leyen tied the agreement to Europe&#x27;s wider push to rearm, decarbonize and become more economically independent, suggesting Australia fits into a larger strategic plan.</li></ul><blockquote>When it comes to trade, Europe is open for business. - Ursula von der Leyen</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>European Commission: Von der Leyen framed the pact as part of building an &#x27;independent Europe,&#x27; arguing the EU can rearm and decarbonize while still showing it remains open to trade. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/australia-eu-sign-free-trade-deal-ursula-von-der-leyen-anthony-albanese-export-boost/'>Politico Europe</a>)</li><li>Australian side: Albanese&#x27;s government presented the signing as a lasting institutional partnership with Europe, using linked defense and research agreements to show the relationship is meant to extend beyond tariffs alone. (<a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/eu-deal-boosts-australian-defence-and-science-alliances/'>Michael West</a>)</li><li>National Farmers Federation: President Hamish McIntyre said agriculture should not be traded away and warned that sub-par market access, alongside EU subsidies, would be a double blow for Australian producers. (<a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/farmers-fear-eu-trade-deal-will-shortchange-agriculture/'>Michael West</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/4a71ddd6-bb5c-439c-9a10-ce3ad1d4377f'>EU and Australia clinch trade and security pacts</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/australia-eu-sign-free-trade-deal-ursula-von-der-leyen-anthony-albanese-export-boost/'>Australia and EU seal sensitive trade deal</a> - politico.eu</li><li><a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/eu-deal-boosts-australian-defence-and-science-alliances/'>EU deal boosts Australian defence and science alliances</a> - michaelwest.com.au</li><li><a href='https: